fpu: give the 68881 a register that can hold it

On a host whose widest IEEE type is a double, a 68881 register does not
fit. The UAE core says so plainly — fpu/types.h makes fpu_register a
uae_f64 — and the C99 core says it by omission: its 80-bit case needs an
x87 long double, its quad case is switched off, and everything else falls
through to a double as well. So eleven of the Macintosh's sixty-four
mantissa bits are dropped on the way in, and its exponent, which reaches
1e4932, is clamped to a double's 1e308.

configure has been answering this on ARM by choosing MPFR, which is
correct and costs a malloc and a free per floating-point instruction
(fpuop_general). There is a third answer on any host whose long double is
IEEE binary128 — AArch64, RISC-V, s390x, PowerPC ELFv2 — and it is exact:
a 15-bit exponent with the Macintosh's own bias of 16383, and 112
fraction bits for the Macintosh's 63. Nothing is lost and nothing is
allocated.

That case exists in the tree already, as USE_QUAD_DOUBLE, disabled with
two objections. The second — that no CPU handles the format natively with
conformance to IEEE — has expired: AArch64 does it through libgcc, and
its libm carries real long-double kernels rather than aliases to double.
The first — that the emulator implementation is not correct — was true,
and this fixes it:

  * make_extended() was not the inverse of extract_extended(). It copied
    wrd2 down from bit 31, integer bit included, where binary128 keeps
    the leading bit implicitly; every significand came out shifted one
    place. Measured on a Raspberry Pi: 1.0 went in as 3fff0000 80000000
    00000000 and came back as 3fff0000 c0000000 00000000, which is 1.5,
    and nine of twelve test patterns were lost.

  * make_nan() and make_inf() shared the long-double branch, which sets
    an `empty` padding field binary128's shape does not have — so it did
    not compile — and set the x87's explicit integer bit, which in
    binary128 turns an infinity into a NaN.

  * Unnormalised operands, which the 68881 can produce and binary128
    cannot spell, are now normalised on the way in. Exact: there are 49
    spare fraction bits to shift into.

fpu_selftest_extended(), under FPU_SELFTEST, is what measured all of the
above and what a port can call to find out where it stands in one line.
It is the only property of this file decidable without a Macintosh, and
it is the one that fails silently: System 7.1 reaches the Finder in four
floating-point instructions, so a boot proves nothing about any of this.

